[ghudson@CIE.UOREGON.EDU writes:]
[] Another thing to figure in would be the move_pet option,...
[] Naturally, move_pet would *not* work on {a jabberwocky-sized pet}

hmmm, interesting points. in my simple model of move_pet the move could
take place if you and your pet are both free to move. moving your pet
wasn't thought to be feat of strength but one of mastery. so moving a
dragon should be well within your ability. i haven't resolved the question
of: what to do if your pet is stuck? i would prefer that you be able to
move the pet then, as well. according to the afore-mentioned model you
could _convince_ your sleeping pet dragon to swap places with you by
somehow waking it up just enough to make the move and then having it
fall back to sleep afterwards, in effect. some might not agree ...

you could never swap places with your pet if you were stuck. however,
if you were trapped you might be able to do such a thing and it could
be lethal for the pet, in which case, i'd like to have you suffer a
kill pet penalty.

i agree that pets should _always_ instinctually respect curses which
to me means that a pet would never pick up a cursed item although it
might, grudgingly, stand on a cursed item.

pet stats: i've been known to collect and train several large dogs
making them super-dogs (i.e. > 200Hp) and then intentionally try to
leave bones. in my dungeons, a large dog named Baron is a considerable
threat (much more so than the ghost of a wizard named Spy?).

pet differentiation: i wouldn't mind complete differentiation amongst
all species and classes but as a practical matter i would only consider
making dogs and cats (not d's and f's) superior pets. other monster
pets could acquire dog-like or cat-like abilities thru polymorph but
would normally be inferior pets (i.e. only fight on your behalf, if
possible) since taming would interfere with their instincts. i would
think that a tame dragon is not a safe proposition because of the
possibility that it might inadvertently blast you. to some extent,
there is already significant pet differentiation. alignment could be
a factor as well.

riding pets: eventually there'll be saddles and saddle bags and so
you could ride certain ridable monsters. this should be problematic,
i.e. the pet would need more food else it might throw you off and
attack you due to confusion. it should be conditional, a weak, clumsy,
stupid and unattractive rider might find that the "horse" would not
obey his instructions and so movement would be as if confused. a strong,
clumsy and stupid rider could hurt his "horse", and so on. finally,
it should be profitable, as the "horse" would sometimes fight on your
behalf (like a pet should) or considering a unicorn, you could teleport
while riding the unicorn even if you weren't able to teleport by
yourself. i could imagine alignment also having something to do with
loyalty, and more ...

